2 CONT
2 CONT >>* e)        ''' VIRGINIA''' (1559-1633).  '''married  (1585/6) HAKON Knutsson Hand,''' son of --- (-1595/6).
2 CONT >>* f)         ''' CONSTANTIA''' (1560-1649).  '''married  ([1594]) HENRIK Frankelin,''' son of --- (-1610).
2 CONT >>* g)         '''LUCRETIA''' (1564-).  '''married --- Zelow, son of  ?'''
2 CONT >>* h)        ''' MARGARETA'''- (1618).  '''married  (1592) OLAVIUS Simonis,''' son of --- (-1639).
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/photo/view/4371107?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000088908403923 Extramarital Relationship with Agda Persdotter]
2 CONT
2 CONT Agda Persdotter, daughter  of the wealthy Stockholm citizen with Finnish descent Per Clementsson, † circa 1575 also called Peder in the gate (S: t Nicolai gatekeeper's house in Storkyrkobrinken).
2 CONT
2 CONT What is known about her is that she was very beautiful and therefore bore the nickname Charitas. According to documents in private ownership, Agda is said to have owned a painful and beautiful plant with fine oval face, large deep
2 CONC  blue eyes and light brown hair. Daughter of the wealthy Stockholm citizen with Finnish descent Per Clementsson, † circa 1575 also called Peder in the gate (S: t Nicolai gatekeeper's house in Storkyrkobrinken). Agda broke with the
2 CONC  king and later married Joakim Fleming, † 1573. After his death Agda resumed the relationship with the king and then the daughter Lucretia was born. Then she married a second time, then with the commander at Stegeborg Christoffer 
2 CONC Olofsson Stråle, † 1585. Her son Johan Christoffersson Stråle, † 1640 and is buried in Skänninge church.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children with various mistresses:=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Virginia Eriksdotter Vaasa,''' born 1559-01-01 at Kalmar Castle, † 1633. In 1566 she became king of his father King Erik XIV, the incidents of jealousy for Russian Grand Prince Ivan Ivanovich. Obtained 1585-05-07 by King Joha
2 CONC n III six farms in Västergötland and 1589-04-14 by Duke Carl five homes in Vadsbo here. Married around 1586 with the steward at Kronoberg's castle Håkan Knutsson Hand
2 CONT * '''Constantia Eriksdotter Vasa,''' born 1560-06-13, † 1649-01-24 Odensfors and buried along with her husband in Undenäs old church. Received in the donation church ceremony of two parishes in Boberg's homestead, as well as Bocks
2 CONC jö, a mantal farm, by Duke Karl's letter. Owned together with her husband Ramsnäs farm in Finnerödja sn. Married to the Chief of Police in Hanekind's ward Henrik Frankelin.
2 CONT * '''Lucretia Eriksdotter Vasa,''' born 1564-01-04, † 1572
2 CONT * '''Margareta Eriksdotter''' (1618). married (1592) OLAVIUS Simonis, son of --- (-1639).
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/4371107?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000088908403923 '''Source'''- The Royal Family Vasa]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married 1568-07-04 [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/4371107?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000088908284960 '''Karin Månsdotter,'''] daughter of the soldier Måns Månsson.  Married on 1567-07-13 Stockholm, Wedding:	1568-07-0
2 CONC 4 Stockholm, Stockholm's Storkyrka  4)	Double wedding: At the same time Erik and Karin married Eriks half sister Sofia with Magnus. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children with Karin Månsdotter=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sigrid Vasa,''' born 1566-10-16 at Svartsjö Castle in Sånga parish, † 1633-04-24 Liuksiala. Married 1: o 1597 with the Castle Act at Turku Castle Henrik Claësson Tott. Married 2: o 1609-09-10 at Stockholm Castle with the head
2 CONC master Nils Nilsson Natt and Dag.
2 CONT * '''Gustav Eriksson Vasa,''' born 1568, † 1607.
2 CONT *''' Henrik Eriksson Vasa,''' born 1570, † 1574.
2 CONT * '''Arnold Eriksson Vasa,''' born and † 1572.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Vasa '''Source''' Adelsvapen Genealogies] 
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik XIV 1533-77, Swedish King 1560-68, son of Gustav Vasa and Katarina of Saxony-Lauenburg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Through Arboga's articles in 1561, Erik strengthened the National Assembly and acquired, through the establishment of a Supreme Court, the Supreme Court of the King (High Council), with Jöran Persson in the lead, a tool for his pe
2 CONC rsonal power. He began the expansive Swedish Baltic Sea policy through the acquisition of Estonia in 1561 and came to war with Denmark and Lübeck in 1563, under which he proved to possess military organizational capacity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The domestic policy tightening led to conflict with the brother Johan and the High Court. Erik's illusory suspicion and fierceness led to Sturemorden in 1567. When he married Karin Månsdotter that same year, the nobility made the 
2 CONC dukes upside down. In July 1567 he left Karin Månsdotter in the presence of councilors. rumors were that he had forced the Archbishop Laurentius Petri to carry out the wedding ceremony. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik was deprived of the crown and was then detained at various castles in Sweden and Finland until he was probably murdered at the castle of Örbyhus at the command of Johan III. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik was richly talented, had versatile interests and stood at the height of his formation. He had extensive foreign policy plans that worried the less imaginative father; Here, the idea of ​​freeing Queen Elisabeth I. Erik was ac
2 CONC tually released to her before she became queen and was divorced and supervised by her suspicious half-sister, "Bloody Mary." Brother Johan had to make a preparatory free trip for Erik's account in 1559 to the newly-elected Queen E
2 CONC lizabeth, and in England he was greatly admired for his form-fitting Latin. But the apostasy to Elizabeth was rejected. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik had a number of lovers: Agda Persdotter, Anna Larsdotter, Karin Jakobsdotter, Karin Pedersdotter, Britta and Ingrid; all of them mentioned until 1565 and these women had some sort of semi-functional position in the vicinity o
2 CONC f Eriks. From 1565 it was Karin Månsdotter, who became Erik's mistress and later also a wife. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frillohopen '''Source''' Wikipedia] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedish tradition of eating crayfish was introduced by Erik XIV - he was very fond of crayfish and cultivated them in the moat around Kalmar Castle. When his sister Anna was married, he ordered the fiancé at Nyköping's castle 
2 CONC to procure a large amount of crayfish for the wedding. Until the 19th century there were a large amount of crayfish in Swedish waters, but the rural population did not think they were human food. By contrast, the medieval monks ab
2 CONC ound with crayfishes, especially during the fasting. Cancer was counted as fish, which they were allowed to eat. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to an expert, an opening in 1958 by Erik XIV's grave did not provide proof that the arsenic found was the result of a poisoning. Another expert's conclusion was that "the whole finding thus fully supports the assumption 
2 CONC that Erik XIV has been poisoned with arsenic." 
2 CONT (Source: Focus 98 and Ingvar Andersson - Erik XIV)
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sciencenordic.com/archaeology-denmark-society--culture/archaeologists-reveal-new-finds-from-legendary-swedish-warship/1457310 Archaeologists Reveal New Finds from Legendary Swedish Warship]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mars warship also known as the Makalös (Peerless), which was constructed between 1563 and 1564 was carrying hundreds of soldiers when it exploded in the Baltic Sea in 1564 during the Northern Seven Years’ War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cannons, hand grenades, and up to a thousand soldiers were on board the large Swedish warship when it exploded in the Baltic Sea, 454 years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ship, known as Mars, belonged to the Swedish navy and was one of Northern Europe’s largest and most feared naval vessels used in the Northern Seven Years’ War.
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains were discovered at the bottom of the Baltic Sea in 2011, near to the Swedish island of Öland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The latest discoveries from the wreckage were revealed during a press conference in Öland.
2 CONT
2 CONT “This year, we have come closer to the people aboard. We found more skeletal parts, including a femur with trauma around the knee which we believe to stem  from a sharp-edged weapon,” says maritime archaeologist Rolf Fabricius War
2 CONC ming, who is one of the researchers involved in the investigation.
2 CONT
2 CONT “We also found large guns and a hand grenade. We can see from the wreckage that it was a very intense and tough battle. Between 800 and 1,000 men were on board. That is comparable to the population of an entire medium-sized town a
2 CONC t the time. Most of them died in the explosion or when the ship sank into the watery depths,” he says.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Read More: Researchers discover remains of sunken Swedish warship'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The ship contained silver treasure
2 CONT Researchers had previously discovered silver treasure among the Mars wreckage. This time, one of the most spectacular finds was a large grapnel (grappling hook) an anchor-like hook, which hung from the bowsprits of warships and wa
2 CONC s used to cling onto another ships in order to board it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grapnels are illustrated in historical sources from the 16th century, but no actual surviving examples are known apart from this particular one, says Warming.
2 CONT
2 CONT “It’s totally unique. Together with other exciting finds, it can shed new light on Medieval and Early Modern naval warfare. ,” he says, and adds that the divers also found remains of possible arms and armour, including helmets and
2 CONC  swords.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Read More: The Viking’s grave and the sunken ship'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Danish and Lübeckian soldiers were on board Mars sunk due to a gunpowder explosion at the front of the ship. But shortly before, it had been under attack by Danish and Lübeckian warships according to written sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Soldiers fought with hand grenades, lances, and spears, which they threw down from the masts. The fighting was structured and carefully calculated but an absolute ruckus” says Warming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Danish soldiers, allied with soldiers from Lübeck, managed to defeat the Swedish crew and capture the warship. When the ship exploded and sank, it had three to four hundred Dano-Lübeckian soldiers aboard.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A Swedish change of tactics'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The new examination of the Mars shipwreck provides new insights into the events that took place between Denmark and Sweden during the Northern Seven Years’ War between 1563 and 1570.
2 CONT
2 CONT They have documented a change in Swedish tactics from a focus on close quarter combat to long distance fighting, as indicated by large cannons up to 4.8 metres long.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the large cannons, the Swedish crew did not manage to avoid engaging in close quarter combat with their enemies. The soldiers aboard were positioned underneath a net that covered the deck and was designed to prevent the en
2 CONC emy from jumping on board – a so-called anti-boarding net.
2 CONT
2 CONT “We know about the use of anti-boarding nets in this battle from richly detailed written sources. The Swedish Admiral, Jacob Bagge, describes in his own account of the battle how he was injured in the shoulder by a javelin thrown 
2 CONC from one of the fighting tops of the enemy ships. t. We are told he became furious and began shooting at those who had injured him with arquebuses so that they fell into the net below,” says Warming.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Read More: Medieval shipwreck hints at psychological warfare'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''A snapshot of a moment in time'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Until 2011, historians relied on written sources for information about what happened to Mars, including letters from the Danish admiral Herluf Trolle and the Swedish admiral Jacob Bagge, and official royal documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the shipwreck provides an entirely different type of documentation.
2 CONT
2 CONT “There’s some ‘fake news’ in the written sources. Many people wanted to claim the honour of defeating the Mars for various political reasons. But when we study the wreckage itself we see a large congruency between the wreck site a
2 CONC nd the historical sources. One of the most striking observations was that it really was sunk by a large explosion. It was so violent that the front of the ship lies 40 metres away from the other remains,” says Warming.
2 CONT
2 CONT For maritime archaeologist Mikkel Thomsen from the Viking Ship Museum, Denmark, looking at the well preserved, and complete remains captures a snapshot of a moment in time.
2 CONT
2 CONT “You really feel that you’re in the killing fields,” says Thomsen, who was not involved in the study.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The wreckage gives a snapshot of a piece of military and political history. It’s also an international history as the Seven Years’ War was fought across national boundaries,” he says.
2 CONT
