2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Presentation.aspx?id=12404 Katarina, by Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Born:1513-09-24
2 CONT * Death:1535-09-23 - City of Stockholm, Stockholm County (at Stockholm Castle.)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Drottning'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Band 20 (1973-1975), page 777.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Merits'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Katarina, b 24 Sept 1513, d 23 Sept 1535 at Sthlm's castle. Parents: Duke Magnus I of Saxony-Lauenburg and Catherine of Braun-schweig-Wolfenbüttel.
2 CONT
2 CONT G 24 Sept 31 in Sthlms Storkyrka m Gustav I in his 1st marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to secure his young royal house and sanction his entry into the circle of princes, Gustav I sought - according to the custom of the time - a distinguished marriage, in which political influence, not religion, was decisive
2 CONC . When the hope of winning a princess from Catholic Poland disappeared in 1528, he turned to the 15-year-old K in ancient Saxony-Lauenburg, where the Reformation was introduced but the Duchess and her powerful circle of brothers w
2 CONC ere well inscribed in the imperial camp. The son of the Danish king Fredrik I (later Kristian III) was married to K's sister. With Lübeck's willing participation, after three years of varying negotiations, the contract could be si
2 CONC gned with Korsholm's Kalmar County and Öland as life insurance. The wedding was performed by the newly elected Archbishop Laurentius Petri. K gave Sweden an emperor-friendly circle of relatives and 13 Dec 33 a heir to the throne t
2 CONC hrough the birth of his son Erik.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition, K was capricious, bound and reluctant, and the marriage should not have been happy. Surely this image of K is rooted in the malicious propaganda after Erik's removal. Equally unreliable are the rumors sprea
2 CONC d during the Greve feud by the enemies Rostock and Lübeck that K during Kristian's visit to Sthlm sept 35 warned his brother-in-law of Gustav, who found out about this through a spy and therefore killed her with a hammer. Kristian
2 CONC , who then did not have much left for Gustav, has himself denied these allegations. At the festivities for the Danish guest during a dance, K had become nauseous and fell ill; perhaps it was a matter of miscarriage. K's German rel
2 CONC atives remained Gustav's most loyal friends and at the grave opening, no injuries to the skull were found during his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT K seems to have had a nicely built figure - barely 160 cm long - and a beautifully shaped head. No authentic image of her is preserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT Author
2 CONT Ivan Svalenius
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katarina_av_Sachsen-Lauenburg Biography of Catherine of Saxony-Lauenburg, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine was the daughter of Duke Magnus I of Saxony-Lauenburg (died 1543) and Catherine of Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel . She received a Protestant upbringing. The marriage was arranged for political reasons. After hearing in vain 
2 CONC in Poland, Denmark and two German principals, Gustav was advised to enter into negotiations with Saxony-Lauenburg. The duchy was poor but its dynasty was akin to several of Europe's most powerful dynasties, it was a center for leg
2 CONC o nectars that the king needed and it was Protestant, at the same time that Catherine's mother was well seen by the emperor and was able to make imperial contacts. Through this Gustav was able to make contacts with German princes 
2 CONC but also with Denmark, when Katarina's sister, Dorothea of ​​Saxony-Lauenburg , was married to the Danish Crown Prince Kristian, later Kristian III . The marriage also linked Gustav with the old popular child's family from which C
2 CONC atherine originated, through her grandmother Catherine of Pomerania-Wolgast . Negotiations began in 1528, but lasted for several years. She received Korsholm and Kalmar counties and Öland as a life gift .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late summer of 1531, Katarina was escorted to Sweden on a fleet led by her future sister-in-law and brother-in-law Margareta Vasa and Johan of Hoya , and met her future husband when the ship anchored in Strömmen in Septembe
2 CONC r. The wedding ceremony took place in Stockholm on Katarina's 18th birthday and was followed by festivities which for the sake of prestige were made as magnificent as the Swedish court had the opportunity at this time. Shortly the
2 CONC reafter, the king's sibling Ebba Eriksdotter (Vasa )'s daughter Brita was betrothed to the king's favorite Gustav Olsson (Stenbock) and the king's niece Brita Joakimsdotter (Brahe) was betrothed to the national council Birger Nils
2 CONC son (Grip), events where the royal couple hosted and which were the first official celebrations Catherine attended as queen after her wedding.
2 CONT
2 CONT Almost nothing is known about Catherine's personality or her time as queen. No name lists are kept about her staff, although it is believed that she brought with her some chief ladies from Germany and that some of the daughters of
2 CONC  the Council of Ministers were appointed to her chief virgins , including Margareta Eriksdotter (Leijonhufvud) . [ 1 ] In older Swedish literature, Catherine is described as whimsical, cold-hearted and constantly complaining about
2 CONC  everything that was Swedish, and marriage has been described as unfortunate on grounds. [ 2 ] The opening of the grave indicates that she had a "fine" head and was 160 cm long. [ 3 ] Catherine descended in September 1533 with an 
2 CONC heir to the throne, son Erik.
2 CONT
2 CONT During a ball given in Stockholm in September 1535 in honor of her brother-in-law Kristian III of Denmark, when Catherine was probably pregnant, the queen fell so badly when she danced with Kristian that she fell asleep. Complicat
2 CONC ions ensued, and Catherine died the day before her 22nd birthday with her unborn child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine was buried in the Great Church in Stockholm on October 1, 1535, but was only buried on December 21, 1560 in Uppsala Cathedral, with Gustav and his other husband Margareta Eriksdotter (Leijonhufvud) . A grave service is e
2 CONC rected in the so-called Vasakoret behind the altar.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to rumors spread by Lübeck and Rostock during the Count feud , Gustav Vasa should have killed Katarina by striking her in the head with an "ax hammer", a walking stick of silver, after he was told by a spy that Katarina 
2 CONC had slandered him before the Danish king during the dance . [ 3 ] Investigations of Katrina's skull and bone during the 1940s have not shown any injuries that could be linked to such an event. [ 3 ] Kristian III of Denmark himself
2 CONC  stated in a letter about how Catherine had fallen during the dance during his visit to Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT In traditional historiography Katarina has often been described negatively and has been a poor example in contrast to Gustav Vasa's second wife Margareta Leijonhufvud, who has been portrayed in the same stereotypical way as the id
2 CONC eal queen. 
2 CONT
