1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==='''Anna Vasa of Sweden'''===
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: John III King of Sweden
2 CONT Mother: Catherine Jagiellon Queen-Consort of Sweden
2 CONT Birth: 17 May 1568 Eskilstuna, Sodermanland, Sweden
2 CONT Death: 26 February 1625 Strasburg, Prussia (what is now Brodnica, Poland)
2 CONT Burial: Church of Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary, Toruń, Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was the youngest child of Duke John of Finland and Catherine Jagiellon, and sister of King Sigismund III Vasa of Poland 7 Grand Duke of Lithuania. She was born at Eskilstunahus in Sweden just after her family was released fro
2 CONC m captivity at Gripsholm Castle. Her father then ascended the throne of Sweden as John III in 1569. Like her brother Sigismund, Anna was raised a Roman Catholic by her mother and attended Catholic mass.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Several marriages were suggested for Anna. In 1577, there had been discussions to arrange the marriage between Anna and an Austrian Archduke, either Matthias or Maximilian II, but this became impossible after her conversion a year
2 CONC  after her mother's death.[2] When her Catholic aunt Princess Cecilia of Sweden suggested a Catholic royal match for her in 1585, John III replied that Anna had converted to Lutheranism the year before. According to the tradition,
2 CONC  the conversion was inspired by the events at the deathbed of her mother in 1583: her mother, who feared purgatory, was comforted by her Jesuit confessor who assured her that purgatory did not exist and was merely used to warn com
2 CONC mon and simple-minded people. The queen sent the Jesuit away, but it made Anna feel distaste for the falseness of Roman Catholicism.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of her mother, her maternal aunt Queen Anna Jagiellon suggested that she be sent to Poland to be raised there, but was turned down by John III. She had her own court, supervised by her mother's former Mistress of t
2 CONC he Robes, Karin Gyllenstierna; one of her maids-of-honours being her cousin, Princess Sigrid of Sweden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1587, her brother Sigismund was elected King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania as Sigismund III. Her aunt Anna Jagiellon repeated her offer to have Anna with her in Poland and this time John III gave his consent for the sak
2 CONC e of Sigismund: "So that the time now in the beginning would not be too long for your dear lord brother".[2] Anna left with Sigismund for Poland 12 September 1587 and was present at his coronation. During her stay at the Polish co
2 CONC urt, she attracted negative attention by celebrating Lutheran masses with her court.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1589, Anna accompanied Sigismund to the meeting with their father in Reval, Swedish Estonia. She was present during the stormy sessions of Riksråd where King John insisted that Sigismund abdicated the Polish throne and came bac
2 CONC k to Sweden. Swedish councilors protested the idea and furious John promised to persecute them.[2] Erik Sparre asked Anna to intercede as mediator and calm her father.[2] While Sigismund returned to Poland, Anna followed her fathe
2 CONC r back to Sweden, where she spent the following three years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1592, Anna returned to Poland to attend the wedding between her brother and Anna of Austria. She was disliked at the Polish court because of her religion and the influence she had over Sigismund and was suspected for having sup
2 CONC ported her father's failed plan to arrange a Protestant marriage for Sigismund with Christina of Holstein-Gottorp.[2] The Archbishop was so provoked by her Lutheran services that he threatened both Anna and Sigismund with excommun
2 CONC ication. Her sister-in-law Anna of Austria, however, prevented any persecution.[2] In July 1593, she carried her new niece Anna Maria at her baptismal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cardinal Andrew Báthory proposed a marriage for her with the Prince of Transylvania. However Anna engaged herself to marry her father's first cousin Count Gustaf Brahe (1558-1615), son of Per Brahe the Elder and a future general i
2 CONC n Poland.[2] They were raised together at court and was reportedly mutually in love with each other, and there where rumors that they met each other in secret in the home of Brahe's sister Ebba Sparre.[3] In 1587, Gustaf Brahe fol
2 CONC lowed Sigismund and Anna to Poland when Sigismund was elected king there. In 1589, he formally asked Sigismund for Anna's hand, and while he was not given a clear answer, he was not refused, which made him hope that he might be ac
2 CONC cepted.[3] The potential marriage between princess Anna and Gustaf Brahe was fiercely opposed by Duke Charles, who viewed it as a plot of Gustav Brahe to make princess Anna ruling queen regnant of Sweden while her only brother Sig
2 CONC ismund was absent in Poland, and he therefore used their relationship in his libelous chronicle Karlskrönikan.[3] Although it was not the most desirable marriage proposed, Anna declined all other suitors. As time passed, nothing c
2 CONC ame of her intended marriage, and both Anna and Brahe remained unmarried. A definite explanation of this has not been found in historical sources, but Gustaf Brahe remained at the side of Sigismund and Anna all his life and ultima
2 CONC tely followed them to Poland when they left Sweden for good.[3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1592, her brother Sigismund succeeded to the throne of Sweden at the death of their father. In September 1593, Anna returned to Sweden in the company of her brother King Sigismund and her sister-in-law Queen Anna. She 
2 CONC was regarded with distrust by the Papal envoy Germanico Malaspina.[2] During the scandalous riot between Catholics and Protestants during the burial service of her Polish musician Sowka in Riddarholmskyrkan in November 1593, her o
2 CONC wn priest Olaus Simonis participated on the Protestant side. Anna herself visited her uncle Charles, Duke of Södermanland, in Uppsala in February 1594, and attended the anti-Catholic sermon of Ericus Schepperus.[2] Sigismund had p
2 CONC lans to make Anna his regent in Sweden during his stay in Poland. This plan, however, was opposed by Duke Charles, who managed to have the Swedish Council to appoint himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1594, Sigismund returned to Poland, while Anna remained in Sweden. Papal envoy Malaspina had convinced him to leave her behind because of her involvement in the religious riot in Riddarholmskyrkan and reminded him about the Arc
2 CONC hbishop's threat of excommunication.[2] Anna was given an allowance with Stegeborg Castle as residence of her own court with the fiefs of Hammarkind, Björkekind, Östkind and Lösing härad. At Stegeborg, she cultivated her interests
2 CONC  for herbal medicine.[2] In 1595, Anna arranged for the love marriage between her maid of honour Sigrid Brahe and Johan Gyllenstierna.[2] This became a great scandal as Brahe was engaged to another by her family. Duke Charles refu
2 CONC sed to mediate and Anna she finally managed to create a settlement between the families at the Söderköping Riksdag of 1595.[2] The wedding also caused a temporary estrangement occurred in the relationship between Anna and Gustaf B
2 CONC rahe, but they soon reconciled.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Sparre Affair was to be the final break between Anna and Duke Charles. Count Erik Larsson Sparre was a staunch supporter of Sigismund, and Anna kept a box for his wife Ebba Sparre, sister of her head lady in waiting Margareta 
2 CONC Brahe (1559–1638), at Stegeborg, which Duke Charles suspected to contain suspicious documents.[2] When Anna left Stegeborg to attend the death bed of queen dowager Gunilla Bielke in 1597, Duke Charles had her household searched, t
2 CONC he box of Ebba Sparre and the correspondence between Anna and the Sparre couple confiscated, and subjected Ebba Sparre as well as her sister Anna's senior lady in waiting Margareta Brahe to an interrogation regarding the Cryptogra
2 CONC phy he found in Anna's papers, and accused Ebba Sparre of having smuggled in her brother, Anna's alleged lover Gustav Brahe to her.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 1592, Sigismund negotiated marriage between Anna and Margrave John George of Brandenburg but political tensions kept delaying the agreement.[2] In February 1598, Sigismund demanded that Charles allowed Anna to return to Pola
2 CONC nd as her wedding to John George was finally set to Easter that year. Charles attempted to prevent her departure by demanding an inventory of her Swedish property and the promise that it would be confiscated by the state should sh
2 CONC e die unmarried.[2] Anna protested, advised Charles to cooperate with Sigismund and offered herself as a mediator. At the return of Sigismund to Sweden, Anna immediately joined him in the civil war that erupted between Sigismund a
2 CONC nd Charles.[2] She was given the task to mediate, but because of Charles' lack of confidence in her, she was not able to achieve much.[2] After Sigismund's defeat at the Battle of Stångebro in 1598, she left Sweden to live with hi
2 CONC m in Poland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1598, Anna spent the rest of her life in Poland, though she always referred to herself as a Princess of Sweden. Known as Anna of Svecia (Anna of Sweden), she was Protestant member of a Catholic royal family, and acted as a p
2 CONC rotector of the exiled Protestant Swedish loyalists of Sigismund and Polish Protestants.[2] Princess Anna maintained good relationship with and influence upon Sigismund, and she functioned as his political adviser on the affairs o
2 CONC f state, something which made her a controversial figure in Poland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the captivity of Carl Gyllenhielm, an illegitimate son of Duke Charles, in Poland in 1610–13, she gave him much attention. She was given the task to interrogate Gyllenhielm by Sigismund, who listened hidden by a curtain.[2]
2 CONC  She accused Charles of conspiracy to entice conflict between Sigismund and John III, something Gyllenhielm denied.[2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna remained unmarried. In 1602, duke Charles de Gonzaga-Nevers suggested a marriage to a French prince, which was never realized. The negotiations with Johann Georg von Brandenburg were finally discontinued in 1609. The rumors t
2 CONC hat she had a love relationship with the exiled Swedish count Gustaf Brahe, a supporter of Sigismund, have never been confirmed, but it was used by Charles IX, who feared her political influence and in a letter to Sigismund from 1
2 CONC 607 referred to her as: "Your poisonous sister, the creator of all evil and born to the destruction of all lands and the Kingdom".[2] Charles IX once feared that she would marry Tsar Dmitrij of Russia, which was however a false ru
2 CONC mor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund made Anna starosta of Brodnica in 1605 and Golub in 1611. She divided her time between her fief and the court of her brother. Anna's appanage was Strasburg (now Brodnica), a Royal Prussian district in Poland near the Bal
2 CONC tic, where she lived in Golub and Strasburg. She became very respected because of her great learning. She was interested in literature, music, gardening and medicine. She was a specialist in medicinal herbs and kept her own apothe
2 CONC cary. With the help of an Italian assistant, she made her own experiments in herbal medicine.[2] She financed the herbarium of Simon Syrenius.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna was buried at the Church of St. Mary in Toruń, Poland several years after her death, as the Pope had first forbidden the burial of a Protestant in a blessed graveyard in Catholic Poland. Only her nephew, king Władysław IV Vas
2 CONC a, got that decree reversed. He built a beautiful black Dębnik marble tomb monument with a white alabaster figure of his beloved aunt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Vasa_of_Sweden 
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] Academic historians Lars W Ericson (p. 318), Dr. Ulf Sundberg (p. 63 acknowledging other opinions) & Dr. David Norrman (p. 128h)
2 CONT [2] Anna, urn:sbl:18719, Svenskt biografiskt lexikon (art av H. Almquist.), hämtad 2013-12-07.
2 CONT [3] Gustaf Brahe, urn:sbl:18043, Svenskt biografiskt lexikon (art av B. BOËTHIUS.), hämtad 2017-12-29.
2 CONT [4] Tegenborg Falkdalen, Karin, Vasadrottningen: en biografi över Katarina Stenbock 1535-1621, Historiska media, Lund, 2015
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Anteckningar om svenska qvinnor'' (In Swedish)
2 CONT ''Svenskt biografiskt handlexikon'' (In Swedish)
2 CONT http://www.visittorun.pl/386,l2.html#vasa
2 CONT http://www.historiesajten.se/visainfo.asp?id=588
