1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebba_Stenbock '''Ebba Gustavsdotter Stenbock'''] was a Swedish noble.dame, and the sister of queen Katarina Stenbock. She married Clas Eriksson Fleming (Klaus Fleming), governor / dic
2 CONC tator of Finland, in 1573. She led the defense of the stronghold Turku Castle for the loyalists of Sigismund III Vasa during the Siege by Charles IX of Sweden in succession of her then deceased spouse Clas Fleming. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ebba Stenbock was''' the daughter of riksråd Gustaf Olofsson Stenbock and Brita Eriksdotter Leijonhufvud, and the sister of Queen Catherine Stenbock. She was the niece of Queen Margareta Leijonhufvud, and thereby the cousin of 
2 CONC the royal children of that marriage, including the future John III of Sweden and Charles IX of Sweden. In 1573, she married Clas Eriksson Fleming (''Klaus Fleming'') in the house of her sister, the Queen Dowager in Stockholm, and 
2 CONC moved with him to Finland. In 1594, her spouse was appointed Governor-General of Finland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ebba Stenbock was''' noted for her fearless conduct towards Duke Charles. A civil war erupted in 1595 when Sigismund III Vasa was challenged by his uncle, the future Charles IX of Sweden. Stenbock's spouse, Klaus Fleming, was o
2 CONC ne of the strongest supporters of Sigismund III Vasa for the throne, and opposed to Charles IX of Sweden by holding the then Swedish province of Finland against Duke Charles. In parallel, the Cudgel War erupted in Finland in 1596.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In April 1597''', after having subdued the Cudgel War and preparing to resist the expected invasion of Duke Charles, Stenbock's spouse Klaus Fleming died. He was succeeded as governor by Arvid Stålarm the Younger, but Stenbock 
2 CONC took over the moral leadership and decided to continue the line of her spouse and defend the Turku Castle against the army of Duke Charles with the help of two noble officers, who took the military leadership by her side. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''She prepared the''' castle for a siege awaiting relief from King Sigismund in Poland. In August 1597, Duke Charles and his army invaded Finland, took Åland, which was the fief of her sister Queen Dowager Catherine, and besieged
2 CONC  Turku Castle. The Duke offered the women of the Castle safe exit, including Ebba, but in vain. During the siege, he fired through the windows of the castle to frighten the female entourage of Stenbock, but they would not allow th
2 CONC emselves to be afraid, as Lady Ebba spoke to them and asked them to show courage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eventually Stenbock was''' forced to surrender, as no help from Poland arrived. Fleming had still not been buried, and his corpse remained in the castle. A legend has it, Duke Charles wanted the coffin opened to reassure himsel
2 CONC f that Fleming was indeed dead. He then pulled dead Fleming's beard, saying. "If you were alive, your head would be loose" upon which Ebba Stenbock replied, "If my blessed husband were alive, Your Grace would never even have made 
2 CONC it in here."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''This scene is''' captured in the famous painting by Albert Edelfelt, which is in the profile picture of Stenbock's. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ebba and her''' children were then taken as prisoner and brought to Stockholm, where they were separated and placed in house arrest. Ebba was placed in a house belonging to Welam de Wijk, captain of Duke Charles' fleet, and in 
2 CONC 1598, de Wijk was arrested for attempt to start a mutiny, encouraged by Ebba, who had promised him one of her daughters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1599, a''' messenger of Sigismund from Finland carrying letters to the loyalists, including Stenbock. Her son was executed in the Turku Bloodbath the same year. No formal accusation was brought against her, and she and her d
2 CONC aughters were released after the Linköping Bloodbath. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Like many other''' wives and daughters of the exiled loyalists to Sigismund, whose estates had been confiscated by Charles, they finally found a refuge with the Queen Dowager Catherine Stenbock at Strömsholm. <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
