1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karin_Gyllenstierna '''Karin Göransdotter Gyllenstierna'''] (before 1538 - died between 1602 and 1605) was a Swedish courtier. She served as hovmästarinna (senior lady in waiting, or 
2 CONC mistress of the Robes) to queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Jagellon Catherine Jagellon] and, after her death, to Princess [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Vasa_of_Sweden Anna Vasa of Sweden].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Private life===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karin was the''' daughter of riksråd Göran Eriksson Gyllenstierna of Fogelvik (- 1575) and Kerstin Nilsdotter Grip (- 1538). She was thereby a relative of the famous [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_Gyllenstierna Christ
2 CONC ina Gyllenstierna]. She married Nils Månsson [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natt_och_Dag Natt och Dag] in 1553. She was thereby the sister-in-law of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margareta_Birgersdotter_Grip Margareta Birgersdott
2 CONC er Grip], who were also her neighbor on the estate Bro. She became a widow in 1554, and gave birth to her son Nils Nilsson Natt och Dag (1554-1613) posthumously. As the guardian of her son, she managed his estate until he was decl
2 CONC ared of legal majority in 1573.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1566, she''' became engaged to riksråd Ivar Ivarsson Liljeörn. She never married him, but the couple consummated their engagement and had a son, Ivar Ivarsson (1567-1590). In 1567, her fiancé was murdered during the [https:/
2 CONC /en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sture_murders Sture Murders]. Despite never being married to Liljeörn, she took control of his property, considering it the inheritance of herself and her son after her betrothed. This entangled her in a law
2 CONC suit over the inheritance with his relatives, which lasted for the rest of her life. In 1587, the King declared his support for her right in the dispute. This did not end it, however. In 1594, her rights were for a second time con
2 CONC firmed over that of the relatives of her late fiancé, but again without the feud being put to an end.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Court life===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karin Gyllenstierna was''' given the position of senior lady in waiting and Mistress of the Robes, or hovmastarinna (Court Mistress), to queen Catherine Jagellon. After the death of the queen in 1583, she was given the equivale
2 CONC nt position at the court of Princess Anna Vasa of Sweden. She is mentioned in this position in 1587, when the next Mistress of the Robes to the (new) queen of Sweden is also recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1591, Anna''' is recorded having a new court mistress. Her son Nils had the position of ceremony master at the court of first queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunilla_Bielke Gunilla Bielke] and then the next queen, [http
2 CONC s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_of_Holstein-Gottorp Christina of Holstein-Gottorp], and her granddaughter, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_Natt_och_Dag Christina Natt och Dag] was, in 1639, appointed joint foster moth
2 CONC er to queen [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina,_Queen_of_Sweden Christina of Sweden] alongside [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebba_Leijonhufvud Ebba Mauritzdotter Leijonhufvud].
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The year of''' her death is not known. She was still alive in 1602, when her son Nils acted as her representative in her endless inheritance dispute with the family of her late fiancé, but she was dead by 1605, when her son act
2 CONC ed in his own name in the same dispute. At that point, she was stated to be dead.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
